{
"text": "In the summer of nineteen fifty nine, a young geologist named Eduardo Semenza was mapping the slopes of a mountain in the Italian Alps. Below him, one of the tallest dams in the world was almost finished. The engineer who had designed that dam was his father. What Eduardo found in the rock suggested that an entire mountain side was not as solid as it appeared. It bore the scars of an enormous prehistoric landslide, one that had already fallen thousands of years earlier and could move again. Four years later, on the night of october ninth, nineteen sixty three, it did. Around two hundred sixty million cubic meters of rock slid into the lake behind the dam in less than a minute. An enormous wave surged across the reservoir, poured over the top of the dam and destroyed the town below. Almost two thousand people were killed. The dam itself survived with remarkably little damage. It is still standing to day. This is the story of Vagent and of how a structure could be engineered with extraordinary precision and still stand at the center of the deadliest landslide disaster in Europe's recorded history.",
